[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that are congruent to 1 modulo 10. Such divisors must be coprime to 10, meaning they cannot be divisible by 2 or 5. Therefore, the divisors must be composed of powers of 3 and 7.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m The possible divisors are of the form \(3^b \times 7^d\) where \(0 \leq b \leq 4\) and \(0 \leq d \leq 1\). We then check which of these divisors are congruent to 1 modulo 10.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m The divisors are:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - For \(d = 0\): \(1, 3, 9, 27, 81\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - For \(d = 1\): \(7, 21, 63, 189\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m Checking these divisors modulo 10:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(1 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(3 \equiv 3 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(9 \equiv 9 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(27 \equiv 7 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(81 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(7 \equiv 7 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(21 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(63 \equiv 3 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(189 \equiv 9 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m - \(567 \equiv 7 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m The divisors that are congruent to 1 modulo 10 are \(1, 21, 81\). Summing these, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m \[ 1 + 21 + 81 = 103 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m Thus, the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1 is \(\boxed{103}\).
