[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m To compute the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1, we start by noting that \(9! = 362880\). The prime factorization of \(9!\) is \(2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that end with the digit 1. These divisors must be congruent to 1 modulo 10. We consider the divisors of the form \(2^a \times 3^b \times 5^c \times 7^d\) where \(a, b, c, d\) are non-negative integers and \(c\) and \(d\) can only be 0 or 1.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m For the divisors to end with 1 modulo 10, we need the product \(2^a \times 3^b \times 7^d\) to be congruent to 1 modulo 10 when \(c=0\) and when \(c=1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m After detailed analysis, we find that the only divisors are 1 and 21. The divisor 1 is trivial, and 21 is another such divisor. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=372176)[0m 
